WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.186
In the last session the conclusion was that when variation is in kind rather than in amount

2
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:15.000
the answer is a custom component: branching that ships inside your binary and goes through code review

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.279
rather than structure an agent composes.

4
00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:18.647
This session builds one.

5
00:00:18.647 --> 00:00:22.181
A component called Position Row, which takes a symbol, a value

6
00:00:22.181 --> 00:00:25.543
an instrument kind and a detail line, and renders an equity

7
00:00:25.543 --> 00:00:27.424
an option and a bond differently.

8
00:00:27.424 --> 00:00:32.325
By the end it is registered, the agent is sending it, and forty of them are on screen.

9
00:00:32.325 --> 00:00:35.460
Everything here compiles and runs; nothing is sketched.

10
00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.768
Start with what the renderer asks of you. A2UI Component is four members.

11
00:00:39.768 --> 00:00:45.139
A name, which is the string the agent puts in the component field, so this is the contract.

12
00:00:45.139 --> 00:00:50.037
A description. A list of properties, which is the schema this component advertises.

13
00:00:50.037 --> 00:00:52.044
And a composable content function.

14
00:00:52.044 --> 00:00:58.064
There is also an optional is ready, which lets a component decline to draw until its data has arrived.

15
00:00:58.064 --> 00:01:03.729
Notice what is not here: nothing about Material, nothing about theming, no base class to extend.

16
00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:08.451
It is an interface, and a Kotlin object satisfies it. One more thing about name.

17
00:01:08.451 --> 00:01:14.707
It is the only coupling between your app and the agent, and it is a string, so treat it like a public API.

18
00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:19.133
Namespace it if you expect more than a handful, get it right the first time

19
00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:24.799
and remember that renaming it is a breaking change for every payload already written against it.

20
00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:29.453
The properties list is where most of the thinking goes, and it is where the first real trap lives.

21
00:01:29.453 --> 00:01:32.779
The obvious move is to construct a property class directly.

22
00:01:32.779 --> 00:01:36.443
That does not compile, because the concrete classes are internal.

23
00:01:36.443 --> 00:01:42.924
You build them through factories on A2UI Property instead, and the factory you pick is a decision, not a formality.

24
00:01:42.924 --> 00:01:50.477
The static ones take a value fixed when the component is sent: string, number, boolean, string enum, component id, child list, action.

25
00:01:50.477 --> 00:01:57.917
The dynamic ones may instead carry a path that binds into the data model: dynamic string, dynamic number, dynamic boolean and so on.

26
00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:03.328
Only dynamic properties can be bound, and only bound properties recompose when the data changes.

27
00:02:03.328 --> 00:02:07.950
Choosing static for something an agent needs to bind is the first bug you will hit

28
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.530
and the symptom is a component that renders once with the right value and then never updates again.

29
00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:18.096
For our row, all four are dynamic, because every one of them comes from the data.

30
00:02:18.096 --> 00:02:22.774
There is a second decision hidden in each factory call, which is the required flag.

31
00:02:22.774 --> 00:02:27.283
Marking a property required does not make the renderer throw when it is missing.

32
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:33.596
It goes into the JSON schema this component advertises, which is what an agent reads to know how to address you.

33
00:02:33.596 --> 00:02:40.980
So required is a statement to whoever writes payloads, not a runtime guard, and you still need a sensible default on the read side.

34
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.798
That is why every bind call here ends in an elvis.

35
00:02:45.253 --> 00:02:49.766
Now the content function, and the technique that matters more than any individual signature.

36
00:02:49.766 --> 00:02:56.072
Write the content function the way you would guess it: an override taking a scope, some properties and a modifier.

37
00:02:56.072 --> 00:03:01.770
Build it, and the compiler says content overrides nothing, and then it does something genuinely useful.

38
00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.651
It prints the signature it wanted.

39
00:03:03.651 --> 00:03:07.910
Content is an extension function on A2UI Component Scope, not a plain method.

40
00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.846
That also explains the second error

41
00:03:09.846 --> 00:03:13.939
because bind was unresolved: inside an extension the scope is the receiver

42
00:03:13.939 --> 00:03:17.922
so its members are already available and passing it explicitly is wrong.

43
00:03:17.922 --> 00:03:22.790
This is worth internalising, because there is no API reference site for these libraries.

44
00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.003
The compiler error is the documentation.

45
00:03:25.003 --> 00:03:28.487
Guess the shape, build it, and copy what it prints back at you.

46
00:03:28.487 --> 00:03:31.640
I wrote this component that way and it took three builds.

47
00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:36.951
It is worth saying why guessing is a reasonable strategy here rather than a sign of bad tooling.

48
00:03:36.951 --> 00:03:44.750
These libraries are alpha, the signatures are still moving, and any reference you found written down would be out of date before you used it.

49
00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:50.005
The compiler is reading the actual artifact on your disk, so it is never wrong and never stale.

50
00:03:50.005 --> 00:03:55.371
Build early and build often, and treat a red error as the fastest documentation lookup available.

51
00:03:58.006 --> 00:03:59.909
So here is the corrected version.

52
00:03:59.909 --> 00:04:04.579
Override fun, A2UI Component Scope dot Content, taking properties and a modifier.

53
00:04:04.579 --> 00:04:06.193
And then four calls to bind.

54
00:04:06.193 --> 00:04:13.918
Note where bind lives: it is an extension on the properties, taking the property you declared, and it returns a nullable of that type.

55
00:04:13.918 --> 00:04:17.608
So properties dot bind of symbol, with an elvis for the default.

56
00:04:17.608 --> 00:04:22.681
Each of those reads the data model and recomposes this component when that path changes.

57
00:04:22.681 --> 00:04:27.005
That is the entire binding layer as far as a component author is concerned.

58
00:04:27.005 --> 00:04:33.001
One detail in that elvis worth noticing: bind returns a nullable even for a property you marked required

59
00:04:33.001 --> 00:04:36.633
because required is a schema statement rather than a guarantee.

60
00:04:36.633 --> 00:04:44.243
If an agent omits it you get null, and a component that crashes on a missing field is a component that takes the whole surface down.

61
00:04:44.243 --> 00:04:45.569
Default, do not assert.

62
00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:49.477
And once the values are bound, the rest is not A2UI at all.

63
00:04:49.477 --> 00:04:52.605
This is a Row, a Column, two Texts and a when expression.

64
00:04:52.605 --> 00:04:56.116
It is ordinary Compose, and that is the point worth dwelling on.

65
00:04:56.116 --> 00:05:03.084
The branching on instrument kind, options in purple, bonds in teal, equities in the default colour, is code in your repository.

66
00:05:03.084 --> 00:05:04.620
It compiles into the binary.

67
00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.186
It goes through your review, your tests and your release process.

68
00:05:08.186 --> 00:05:09.613
An agent cannot change it.

69
00:05:09.613 --> 00:05:17.130
What an agent can do is say render a Position Row with this data, which is the same class of statement as render a Text with this string.

70
00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:23.988
That is why this satisfies a no runtime generation constraint while still letting one component cover three instrument types.

71
00:05:23.988 --> 00:05:28.267
And because it is ordinary Compose, everything you already know still applies.

72
00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:32.273
You can use your own theme, your own typography, your own spacing tokens.

73
00:05:32.273 --> 00:05:35.729
You can call into existing composables from your design system.

74
00:05:35.729 --> 00:05:41.271
The modifier parameter is passed in by the renderer and you should apply it to your outermost element

75
00:05:41.271 --> 00:05:43.849
because that is how the parent controls layout.

76
00:05:43.849 --> 00:05:48.348
Forget that and your component will ignore the width the surface tried to give it.

77
00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:49.525
The component exists.

78
00:05:49.525 --> 00:05:55.182
Getting the renderer to find it is where I lost the most time, so this part is worth doing carefully.

79
00:05:55.182 --> 00:06:01.367
My first attempt was to put the component in its own catalog and hand the processor both, Material's and mine.

80
00:06:01.367 --> 00:06:06.146
It compiled, it ran, and the row rendered as a red error chip with nothing in logcat.

81
00:06:06.146 --> 00:06:09.182
The reason is in the protocol rather than the library.

82
00:06:09.182 --> 00:06:12.218
Create surface carries a single catalog id, one string

83
00:06:12.218 --> 00:06:17.560
so a surface resolves against exactly the catalog it names and anything missing fails silently.

84
00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:21.551
What works is building one catalog that extends Material's: your own id

85
00:06:21.551 --> 00:06:25.600
Material's components plus yours, Material's functions and theme schema.

86
00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.230
Then the agent names your id.

87
00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:31.278
Version one point zero adds mixable catalogs and removes this constraint

88
00:06:31.278 --> 00:06:33.640
but on zero point nine this is the pattern

89
00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:37.125
and the silent failure is what makes it expensive to discover.

90
00:06:37.125 --> 00:06:41.567
Worth dwelling on how that failure presented, because it is the expensive part.

91
00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.366
No exception. Nothing in logcat.

92
00:06:43.366 --> 00:06:49.382
Just a small red chip where the row should be, which is the same thing you see when an enum value is wrong.

93
00:06:49.382 --> 00:06:51.856
So when a component renders as an error chip

94
00:06:51.856 --> 00:06:56.748
the two things to check in order are whether the catalog naming it actually contains it

95
00:06:56.748 --> 00:07:00.346
and whether every enum value you sent is one the catalog allows.

96
00:07:00.346 --> 00:07:02.352
In the app that is a one line change.

97
00:07:02.352 --> 00:07:07.394
Wrap the Material catalog in extended catalog, and hand the processor a list containing that.

98
00:07:07.394 --> 00:07:12.599
Everything the Material catalog could render still renders, because its components are in there.

99
00:07:12.599 --> 00:07:14.388
And now Position Row renders too.

100
00:07:14.388 --> 00:07:19.755
Note also what did not change: the Material components are still resolving through the same catalog

101
00:07:19.755 --> 00:07:23.875
because we kept its component list and added to it rather than replacing it.

102
00:07:23.875 --> 00:07:30.923
Getting that wrong, by passing only your own components, would make every Text and Column on the surface fail the same silent way.

103
00:07:30.923 --> 00:07:32.812
Which changes what the agent sends.

104
00:07:32.812 --> 00:07:38.808
Before, a row was three basic components: a Row containing two Texts, with the layout expressed in the payload.

105
00:07:38.808 --> 00:07:42.698
Now it is one component, named Position Row, with four bound properties.

106
00:07:42.698 --> 00:07:46.803
The payload got smaller and, more importantly, it stopped describing layout.

107
00:07:46.803 --> 00:07:51.071
The agent no longer says put the symbol on the left and the value on the right.

108
00:07:51.071 --> 00:07:55.285
It says here is a position, and the client decides what a position looks like.

109
00:07:55.285 --> 00:08:00.525
That is a better division of responsibility, and it is also fewer things for a reviewer to check.

110
00:08:00.525 --> 00:08:02.523
There is a governance angle here too.

111
00:08:02.523 --> 00:08:07.223
A payload that names Position Row is shorter to review than one that describes a layout

112
00:08:07.223 --> 00:08:11.005
and it cannot express anything the component does not already support.

113
00:08:11.005 --> 00:08:13.165
The schema you declared is the boundary.

114
00:08:13.165 --> 00:08:16.623
An agent cannot send a fifth property and have it mean something

115
00:08:16.623 --> 00:08:21.485
which is exactly the property you want when someone asks what a payload could possibly do.

116
00:08:21.485 --> 00:08:26.757
Here is the finished file in Android Studio, resolved and indexed, which is worth seeing whole.

117
00:08:26.757 --> 00:08:28.865
The four dynamic properties at the top

118
00:08:28.865 --> 00:08:31.751
the name and description that form the wire contract

119
00:08:31.751 --> 00:08:36.246
the properties list that is the schema, the content extension with its four binds

120
00:08:36.246 --> 00:08:37.689
and the layout underneath.

121
00:08:37.689 --> 00:08:42.017
Ninety five lines including comments, and that is a complete custom component.

122
00:08:42.017 --> 00:08:48.510
And notice the inlay hints the IDE is showing: parameter names on every factory call, and usage counts in the gutter.

123
00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:54.393
None of that works without a successful Gradle sync, which is the one thing worth fixing before you start.

124
00:08:54.393 --> 00:08:55.353
So let us see it.

125
00:08:55.893 --> 00:08:57.641
One holding, and it is an option.

126
00:08:57.641 --> 00:09:02.569
The symbol on the left with the contract detail underneath, the value on the right in purple.

127
00:09:02.569 --> 00:09:04.689
None of that layout came from the agent.

128
00:09:04.689 --> 00:09:10.146
The payload said Position Row with four values, and everything you see is the composable we just wrote.

129
00:09:10.146 --> 00:09:11.975
And here is forty of them, mixed.

130
00:09:11.975 --> 00:09:17.130
Equities plain, options purple with a contract line, bonds teal with a coupon and a maturity.

131
00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:22.118
One component, one wire name, one property schema, three visual treatments chosen by data.

132
00:09:22.118 --> 00:09:28.436
If you had done this with basic catalog components it would be three templates and a selection rule on the server.

133
00:09:28.436 --> 00:09:35.696
Here it is one component and a kind field, and the decision about what a bond looks like lives in the app where it can be reviewed.

134
00:09:36.574 --> 00:09:40.377
To close, the honest accounting. The component is ninety five lines.

135
00:09:40.377 --> 00:09:42.110
Getting there took three builds

136
00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:45.466
all of them fixing signatures that the compiler then told me

137
00:09:45.466 --> 00:09:51.226
and one silent failure from the catalog rule that took longer than all the compile errors put together.

138
00:09:51.226 --> 00:09:57.378
The real cost is not this file, it is that a custom component is a renderer change on every platform you ship.

139
00:09:57.378 --> 00:10:01.014
One component here is one component on iOS and one on the web too

140
00:10:01.014 --> 00:10:05.823
and they have to agree on the property schema or the same payload renders differently.

141
00:10:05.823 --> 00:10:13.597
So the rule of thumb is the one from the last session: reach for this when one component replaces several templates, and not for a one off.

142
00:10:13.597 --> 00:10:17.512
For a team already building its own renderers, this is not extra work.

143
00:10:17.512 --> 00:10:22.154
It is the thing you were going to build anyway, pointed at a problem worth solving.
